The file `data.txt` contains thousands of lines of words paired with passwords, making it impossible to search manually. The `grep` command solves this — it searches through a file and returns only the lines that match a given word or pattern. Running `grep "millionth" data.txt` instantly finds the one line containing the word `millionth`, revealing the password right next to it.

`grep` searches for a pattern inside a file or output.
Basic syntax: `grep <pattern> <file>`

Key flags:
| Flag | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `-i` | Case-insensitive search |
| `-r` | Search recursively through directories |
| `-n` | Show line numbers of matches |
| `-v` | Invert match — show lines that do NOT match |
| `-c` | Count the number of matching lines |
| `-l` | Show only filenames that contain the match |


**Level 8 - Level 9: The password for the next level is stored in the file data.txt and is the only line of text that occurs only once**

ssh bandit8@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220
password: dfwvzFQi4mU0wfNbFOe9RoWskMLg7eEc

bandit8@bandit:~$ ls
data.txt
bandit8@bandit:~$ sort data.txt | uniq -u
4CKMh1JI91bUIZZPXDqGanal4xvAg0JM

`data.txt` contains many lines of passwords, most of which are duplicates — the goal is to find the only line that appears once. Using `sort` first groups all identical lines together, then piping into `uniq -u` filters down to only the unique line. Running `sort data.txt | uniq -u` returns the password instantly.

`sort` arranges lines of a file in a specific order.
Basic syntax: `sort <file>`

| Flag | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `-r` | Reverse order (Z to A) |
| `-n` | Sort numerically instead of alphabetically |
| `-u` | Sort and remove duplicates in one step |
| `-k` | Sort by a specific column e.g. `-k2` sorts by second column |
| `-f` | Case-insensitive sort |

`uniq` filters out or reports repeated lines. Important: it only detects 
duplicates on **adjacent lines**, which is why `sort` must always be used before it.
Basic syntax: `uniq <file>`

| Flag | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `-u` | Show only lines that appear exactly once |
| `-d` | Show only duplicate lines |
| `-c` | Prefix each line with the number of times it appears |
| `-i` | Case-insensitive comparison |


**Level 9 - Level 10: The password for the next level is stored in the file data.txt in one of the few human-readable strings, preceded by several ‘=’ characters.**

  ssh bandit9@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220
  password: 4CKMh1JI91bUIZZPXDqGanal4xvAg0JM

bandit9@bandit:~$ ls
data.txt
bandit9@bandit:~$ grep "=" data.txt
grep: data.txt: binary file matches
bandit9@bandit:~$ file data.txt
data.txt: data
bandit9@bandit:~$ strings data.txt | grep "=="
 ========== the
========== password
========== is
========== FGUW5ilLVJrxX9kMYMmlN4MgbpfMiqey

Before diving in, it's always good practice to run `file data.txt` to understand what type of file you're dealing with. In this case, it returns `data` meaning it's a binary file, not plain text. This is further confirmed when running `grep "=" data.txt` directly, which throws a "binary file matches" error — grep detects binary content and refuses to print garbled output cleanly.

Since the file is binary, we use the `strings` command first to extract all human-readable text from it, then pipe it into `grep "=="` to filter for lines containing `==`, which hints at the password location. Running `strings data.txt | grep "=="` reveals the password across four lines.

`strings` command extracts all printable/human-readable character sequences from any file, including binary files.
Basic syntax: `strings <file>`

| Flag | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `-n <number>` | Set minimum string length to display e.g. `-n 8` only shows strings of 8+ characters (default is 4) |
| `-a` | Scan the entire file, not just certain sections |
| `-o` | Print the offset (position in file) of each string |
| `-t x` | Print offset in hexadecimal format |
| `-e` | Specify character encoding e.g. `-e b` for 16-bit big-endian |

`strings` is especially useful in CTFs and malware analysis when inspecting binary files for hidden text, hardcoded passwords, or URLs.







